In article <eo7ovq$isn$1@news.albasani.net>, Ohle Claussen says...
> On 2007-01-12, dogscoff wrote:
> > On a similar note, a rather handy little exploit occurred to me the
> > other day: Normal armour almost never polymorphs to magical armour. I
> > think this is a deliberate feature on the nh devteam's part to make it
> > quite difficult to convert +7 elven stuff to +7 speedboots/ dex gloves/
> > hoBrilliance etc.
> > However, many of [s]'s magical armours can be acquired by upgrading
> > mundane items - so just polypile your +7 (or higher, if you have lots
> > of scrolls to burn) elven stuff until you get non-magical leather
> > gloves or dwarvish helms, then dip them up to magical status. Feature
> > or bug?
> Or yet another example of SlashEM's feeping crea^W^W creeping featurism
> offsetting game balance? The SlashEM philosophy seems to be to introduce
> both features giving the player more power and features making it easier
> for the RNG to smite the player, to keep the game difficulty roughly
> constant while pushing the complexity up, instead of considering the
> balance issues of each feature separately. At least, that's my impression
> as a NetHacker who has only tried SlashEM a few times.

I think your summing up of the general style differences is rather on 
the spot. I see Slash'EM as a fun and interesting playground for ideas 
that *might* (some of them at least) be suitable for Nethack. I play 
about 50/50 of S and N these days. N to do 'the same old things' in new 
ways (this is positive!) and to improve my skills, S to be surprised by 
what can happen and to 'explore' things I'm not spoiled about.

/Kristoffer

